

Astrometric Proton Propulsion Simulation Engine (APP‑SE)

One‑Page Scientific Brief

Author: C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent Designer, Principal Architect

Overview

The Astrometric Proton Propulsion Simulation Engine (APP‑SE) is a research‑grade simulation architecture for modeling laser‑driven proton propulsion inside astrophysically realistic environments. It integrates plasma physics, cosmic‑scale field ingestion, magnetic nozzle dynamics, and high‑performance computing into a unified propulsion simulation instrument.

Scientific Motivation

Conventional propulsion simulations assume isolated laboratory conditions. APP‑SE challenges this by embedding the engine inside cosmic structure fields, enabling investigation of how interstellar density, magnetic topology, cosmic‑ray flux, and turbulence influence proton acceleration and thrust formation. This approach reframes propulsion as a cosmic‑embedded physics system rather than a closed mechanical device.

Core Physics

APP‑SE models proton motion using the Lorentz force:

[ F = q(\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) ]

Key mechanisms include:

TNSA — electron‑sheath acceleration

RPA — radiation‑pressure acceleration

Magnetic nozzle shaping — beam collimation, divergence control, thrust vectoring

PIC plasma evolution — particle‑in‑cell modeling of laser–plasma interaction

Maxwell solver (Yee lattice) — electromagnetic field propagation

Collision operator — Coulomb and Bremsstrahlung interactions

These components allow APP‑SE to simulate proton beam formation with high fidelity.

Cosmic Data Ingestion

APP‑SE incorporates astrophysical datasets (HDF5, FITS, GEANT4) including:

Cosmic‑ray spectra

ISM density maps

Solar wind telemetry

Galactic magnetic field surveys

The ingestion pipeline performs WCS mapping, normalization, resampling, and noise filtering. These datasets become engine boundary conditions, influencing plasma density, magnetic topology, flux gradients, and environmental turbulence.

Engine Dynamics Module

APP‑SE models:

Proton injection

Acceleration cavity behavior

Magnetic nozzle geometry

Thrust vector control

Beam shaping and stability

Outputs include proton energy spectra, thrust curves, divergence profiles, nozzle efficiency, and stability metrics under cosmic‑scale fields.

HPC Execution

APP‑SE runs on heterogeneous compute systems using:

MPI distributed parallelism

CUDA/HIP GPU acceleration

SLURM/PBS scheduling

Profiling and bottleneck analysis

This enables petascale simulation of proton propulsion physics.

